! jb_af1c F215 "LOAD FACTOR TEST", sub-block F215A "ARITHMETIC CHECKS",
! p.144 (octal 020172.00-020213.40, the block's own exit "BD,$+1.0").
! F215B, the sign-modifier combinations, follows.
!
! THIS BLOCK ADDS LFT (LOAD FACTOR, opcode 1001010) TO THE MODEL -- the
! same situation D* was in a page earlier: in the decoder's valid-op
! list since the opcode tables were transcribed, never in the
! executor's.  Reference Manual p.98: "The operand specified by the
! effective address is placed in the factor register, FT.  The
! accumulator does not participate in the operation and remains
! unchanged."  So it is LOAD with word 14 as the destination, no sign
! byte, the sign-modified sign in FT bit 60, and FT bits 61-63 set to
! zero.
!
! HOW THE BLOCK READS A REGISTER IT CANNOT LOAD THE ACCUMULATOR WITH.
! FT is word 14, so a connective can address it as storage -- and that
! is exactly what these checks do.  Each sub-test first fills the
! accumulator's RIGHT word with the value it expects, then compares FT
! against it:
!
!   CT0110(BU,60), $FT, 4     XOR of FT bits 0-59 against accumulator
!                             IBM 64-123, which is the right word's own
!                             bits 0-59.  Zero result means they match.
!   BZB / BB, $FT+.60         the sign bit on its own, since bit 60 is
!                             outside the 60-bit field above.
!
!   A1  L(BU),FPK0  then LFT(U),FPK0   -- FT 0-59 zero, sign zero.
!   A2  L(BU),FPK1  then LFT(U),FPK1   -- FT 0-59 all ones, sign one.
!   A3  L(BU),FPK1, CM1111(BU,64),$FT, then LFT(U),$FT -- the same
!       answer reached by loading FT FROM ITSELF.  A connective writes
!       ones into word 14 and the very next instruction reads word 14
!       as its storage operand, so this is a store-close-to-fetch
!       across two instruction classes as well as an LFT check.
!
! WHAT F215 DOES NOT COVER, recorded before running rather than after.
! Every sub-test here is %U, so LFT's NORMALIZED path is untested; and
! nothing in F215A or F215B ever looks at FT bits 61-63, so p.98's "FT
! bits 61-63 are set to zero" is manual-only.  The A-checks read bits
! 0-59 and bit 60, and F215B reads bit 60 alone.
!
! TRANSCRIPTION NOTE: the scan prints A2's LFT at 020200.00.  It cannot
! be -- the L above it is at 020177.40 and is a full word, so the LFT
! is at 020200.40, and every one of the nineteen rows below it then
! lands on its printed address through B,SSW at 020215.00.  Same shape
! as F214XB2's misread row.  Transcribed as 020200.40.
!
! The listing's mode-select prologue at 020167.00-020171.40 is SSIP
! framework and is not transcribed; this program starts at SAP19,
! 020172.00.

! -- SSIP framework substitutes: SERS (octal 1304) -> FAIL halt,
! -- SEN (octal 1310) -> scratch.
        SLC, (8)1304.0
SERS    BEW, $
        SLC, (8)1310.0
SEN     DD(BU,64,8), 0

! -- constants (pool p.176) --
        SLC, (8)22005.0
FPK0    DD(BU,64,8), (8)0000000000000000000000
FPK1    DD(BU,64,8), (8)1777777777777777777777

        SLC, (8)20172.0
! F215A1
SAP19   L(BU), FPK0
        LFT(U), FPK0
        CT0110(BU,60), $FT, 4                ! test $FT0-59 +0
        SIC, SEN
        BRGZ, SERS
        BZB, $FT+.60, $+2.0                  ! test sign bit of $FT +0
        SIC, SEN
        B, SERS
! F215A2
        L(BU), FPK1
        LFT(U), FPK1
        CT0110(BU,60), $FT, 4                ! test $FT0-59 +1 s
        SIC, SEN
        BRGZ, SERS
        BB, $FT+.60, $+2.0                   ! test sign bit of $FT +1
        SIC, SEN
        B, SERS
! F215A3 - load FT from FT itself
        L(BU), FPK1
        CM1111(BU,64), $FT
        LFT(U), $FT
        CT0110(BU,60), $FT, 4                ! test $FT 0-59 +1 s
        SIC, SEN
        BRGZ, SERS
        BB, $FT+.60, $+2.0                   ! test sign bit of $FT +1
        SIC, SEN
        B, SERS
PASS    BEW, $                               ! 020213.40: the block's exit "BD,$+1.0"
        END, SAP19
